        "content": "Now let me work through the mathematics carefully.\n\n**Setting up the problem:**\n\nThe condition is $y = \\int \\frac{4y^3}{4-y^6}\\,dx$. This is an integral equation. Differentiating with respect to $x$ (treating $y$ as a function of $x$... but actually here $x = x(y)$ is the function). Let me interpret carefully.\n\nThe relation $y = \\int \\frac{4y^3}{4-y^6}\\,dx$ means: as a function of $x$, $y$ satisfies $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{4y^3}{4-y^6}$.\n\nSince $x = x(y)$ is the inverse function, we have $\\frac{dx}{dy} = \\frac{4-y^6}{4y^3}$.\n\n**Part (1): Arc length**\n\n$\\frac{dx}{dy} = \\frac{4-y^6}{4y^3} = \\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4}$.\n\nArc length: $s = \\int_{-2}^{-1}\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{dx}{dy}\\right)^2}\\,dy$.\n\nLet me compute $1 + \\left(\\frac{4-y^6}{4y^3}\\right)^2 = 1 + \\frac{(4-y^6)^2}{16y^6} = \\frac{16y^6 + (4-y^6)^2}{16y^6}$.\n\n$(4-y^6)^2 = 16 - 8y^6 + y^{12}$.\n\nNumerator: $16y^6 + 16 - 8y^6 + y^{12} = y^{12} + 8y^6 + 16 = (y^6+4)^2$.\n\nSo $\\sqrt{1+(dx/dy)^2} = \\frac{y^6+4}{4|y|^3}$. Since $y \\in [-2,-1]$, $y<0$, $|y|^3 = -y^3$.\n\nSo integrand $= \\frac{y^6+4}{-4y^3} = -\\frac{y^3}{4} - \\frac{1}{y^3}$.\n\n$s = \\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(-\\frac{y^3}{4} - y^{-3}\\right)dy = \\left[-\\frac{y^4}{16} + \\frac{1}{2y^2}\\right]_{-2}^{-1}$.\n\nAt $y=-1$: $-\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{16}$.\nAt $y=-2$: $-\\frac{16}{16} + \\frac{1}{8} = -1 + \\frac{1}{8} = -\\frac{7}{8}$.\n\n$s = \\frac{7}{16} - \\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = \\frac{7}{16} + \\frac{14}{16} = \\frac{21}{16}$.\n\n**Part (2): Maximum of $x$**\n\n$x(y) = \\int \\frac{4-y^6}{4y^3}\\,dy = \\int\\left(\\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4}\\right)dy = -\\frac{1}{2y^2} - \\frac{y^4}{16} + C$.\n\nUsing $x(-1) = -\\frac{9}{16}$: $-\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{16} + C = -\\frac{9}{16} \\Rightarrow -\\frac{8}{16}-\\frac{1}{16}+C = -\\frac{9}{16} \\Rightarrow C = 0$.\n\nSo $x(y) = -\\frac{1}{2y^2} - \\frac{y^4}{16}$.\n\n$x'(y) = \\frac{1}{y^3} - \\frac{y^3}{4} = \\frac{4-y^6}{4y^3}$. On $[-2,-1]$, $y^6 \\in [1,64]$, so $4-y^6 < 0$ and $y^3<0$, thus $x'(y) > 0$. So $x$ is increasing on $[-2,-1]$.\n\nMaximum at $y=-1$: $x(-1) = -\\frac{9}{16}$.\n\nLet me verify: $x(-1) = -\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{16} = -\\frac{9}{16}$. ✓\n\nSo the maximum value of $x$ is $-\\frac{9}{16}$.\n\nLet me submit the solution.",
